# 116 - §6 (.40), short-circuit typing: the LEFT operand of and/or must be
# Bool, and the right is type-checked ONLY if it is evaluated. So `false and 1`
# is false and `true or nil` is true even though neither 1 nor nil is a Bool.
# An engine that type-checked both operands up front - or that compiled the
# rhs check before the jump - would raise a Type error here instead.
--- source
print false and 1;
print true or nil;
--- expect
false
true
